[THOUGHT] AST and ALT are both elevated (AST > ALT), consistent with heat-induced hepatic injury — a classic feature of severe heat stroke. Combined with hyperthermia (104°F), altered mental status, rhabdomyolysis (↑CK), and absence of hypoglycemia or severe electrolyte derangement, the evidence strongly converges on **classic heat stroke**. However, I must still systematically address all eight evidence domains. So far, I have:  
1) Chief complaint & onset → inferred from context (altered mental status + heat exposure);  
2) Course → acute, witnessed undressing in heat;  
3) Associated symptoms → somnolence, paranoia, non-responsiveness — but need to assess for seizures, headache, nausea/vomiting, myalgias, dark urine (rhabdo sign);  
4–6) Past history, meds, social/exposures — completely unknown;  
7) Physical exam — not yet requested;  
8) Labs — partially complete.  

I have not yet gathered *any* past medical/surgical history, medications, allergies, or social context — all critical to rule out confounders (e.g., anticholinergic use, psychiatric illness, substance use, cardiovascular disease). Since the patient is uncooperative, this information must come from collateral sources (EMS, police, records) or observation. But per protocol, I’ll now request the *most clinically urgent missing domain*: targeted physical exam — specifically looking for signs of heat stroke (e.g., hot/dry vs. wet skin — though classic heat stroke may have *some* sweating initially; presence of nuchal rigidity to rule out meningitis; pupillary reactivity; motor tone, clonus, or posturing suggesting seizure or encephalopathy).

[TEST] Neurological examination: pupillary size and reactivity, presence of nuchal rigidity, and assessment for clonus or abnormal posturing

